    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3-0301, En Con L; amd §206, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5615\n\nSPONSOR: BENJAMIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law and\nthe public health law, in relation to establishing environmental stand-\nards for ambient lead and lead contamination in soils and on floors and\nwindow sills\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo protect the public health by enacting stricter environmental stand-\nards for ambient lead and for the prevention of lead contamination of\nsoils and on floors and window sills.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill establishes legislative intent.\n\nSection 2 of the bill adds a new paragraph nn to subdivision one of\nsection 3-0301 of the environmental conservation law directing the\ncommissioner of environmental conservation, in consultation with the\ncommissioner of health, to adopt environmental standards and rules\nhaving the force and effect of standards to carry out the purposes of\nsuch standards, which require:\n\n1. that areas containing lead contaminated soil shall have levels of\nlead contamination not greater than two hundred parts per million for\nresidential zoned areas and one thousand parts per million for areas\nzoned for commercial and/or industrial development; and\n\n2. that a lead dust hazard as defined in part 40 Code of Federal Regu-\nlations section 745.101 shall not be greater than 5 micrograms per\nsquare foot on floors and not greater than 40 micrograms per square foot\nfor window sills; and that clearance standards, as defined in 40 Code of\nFederal Regulations 745.223, shall be set at the same levels; and\n\n3. that the average ambient air quality standard, calculated as a roll-\ning 3 month average, shall not be greater than .075 micrograms per cubic\nmeter.\n\nSection 3 of the bill adds a new subdivision 31 to Section 206 of the\npublic health law directing the commissioner of health, upon request of\nthe commissioner of environmental conservation, to consult with such\ncommissioner on the creation and adoption of environmental standards for\nlead concentrations in lead contaminated soils, lead concentrations on\nfloors and window sills, and lead concentrations in ambient air.\n\nSection 4 of the bill states that the effective date shall be the nine-\ntieth day after it shall have become law, and effective immediately, the\naddition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary\nfor the implementation of this act on its effective date are authorized\nto be made and completed on or before such date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill will make New York a leader in protecting the public and chil-\ndren from the harmful effects of exposure to dust-lead hazards.  It\nestablishes new and stricter environmental standards in New York law for\nambient lead dust and lead contamination in soil and on floors and\nwindow sills. This legislation is necessary in light of the failure of\nthe federal Environmental Protection Agency to promulgate stricter stan-\ndards to protect the public and children from such hazards.\n\nIn 2012 the federal Center for Disease Control (CDC) stated that there\nis no known safe blood lead level for children and determined that 5\nmicrograms per deciliter of blood would be sufficient to trigger a\npublic health response, one-half of the level of 10 micrograms per\ndeciliter that had been targeted by the federal environmental Protection\nAgency as a safe blood lead level for children.\n\nIn 2016 environmental advocates filed a petition for a writ of mandamus\nagainst the federal Environmental Protection Agency in the United States\nCourt of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit seeking to compel the Agency to\nact upon a rule making petition it granted previously concerning dust-\nlead hazard and lead paint materials. (In Re a Community Voice v. US\nEnvironmental Protection Agency). The petitioners stated that although\nthe EPA was mandated by Congress to issue its initial rules identifying\ndust lead hazards by 1992, the Agency did not identify dust lead hazards\nfor child-occupied facilities until 2001. The EPA established standards\nfor floors as 40 micrograms per square foot and for interior window\nsills as 250 micrograms per square foot, which it estimated would result\nin one to five percent chance of child developing a \"safe\" blood level\nof 10 micrograms per deciliter.\n\nThe petitioners further stated that although scientific research had\nfurther advanced the understanding of the dangerousness of lead, the EPA\nhad not changed its standards. In 2009 eight of the petitioners filed an\nadministrative petition with the EPA asking it to use its rule making\nauthority to adequately protect children by lowering the dust-lead\nhazard standards to 10 micrograms per square foot for floors and 100\nmicrograms per square foot for window sills. The EPA granted the peti-\ntioners' request, but since 2009, the petitioners' request, but since\n2009, the petitioners had not heard anything further about their\nrequest.\n\nIn December 2017, the Court of Appeals granted the petition for mandamus\nand held that under the federal Toxic Substances Control Act and the\nResidential Lead-based Paint Hazard Reduction Act the EPA was under a\nduty to update dust-lead hazard standards and lead-based paint standards\nin light of the obvious need and a duty under the federal Administrative\nProcedure Act to fully respond to the petitioners' rule making petition.\n\nOn July 2, 2018 the EPA filed a notice of proposed rule change stating\nthat addressing childhood lead exposure was a priority (Federal Regis-\nter, Vol. 83, No. 127). The EPA proposed that the dust-lead hazard stan-\ndards should be lowered from 40 micrograms per square foot for floors to\n10 micrograms per square foot, and from 250 micrograms per square foot\nto 100 micrograms per square foot for window sills. The EPA took no\naction with respect to lead based paint \"due to insufficient information\nto support a change.\"\n\nThe EPA directed that all comments about the proposed rule changes must\nbe made by August 16, 2018. The EPA has not yet finalized the proposed\nchanges.\n\nIn August 2018 Earthjustice, an environmental advocacy organization,\nfiled comments regarding the proposed rules on behalf of the petition-\ners. It urged the EPA to adopt lower thresholds for lead dust hazards on\nfloors and window sills, and that the EPA should revise the soil lead\nhazard standards. It stated that based upon extensive research and\nstudies, a clearance to dust-lead loading on floors below 5\nmicrograms/square foot and in window sills below 50 micrograms/square\nfoot was achievable. It recommended that the EPA adopt dust-lead hazard\nstandards to be set no higher than 5 micrograms/square foot on floors\nand 40 micrograms/square foot on window sills, and that clearance stand-\nards must be revised to meet these levels. A similar recommendation was\nmade on behalf of 73 individuals and environmental organizations.\n\nThis bill adopts these recommendations for floors and window sills and\nclearance levels.\n\nThe EPA took no action with respect soil-lead hazard, which is defined\nas \"bare soil on residential property or on the property of a child\noccupied facility that contains lead equal to or exceeding 400 parts per\nmillion in a play area or average of 1,200 parts per million of bare\nsoil in the rest of the yard based on soil samples.\"  (40 CFR Part\n745.65 (c)). Earthjustice stated that this standard, set in 2001, was\nlong outdated and must be revised to reflect the current science. A\nsimilar recommendation was made on behalf of 73 individuals and environ-\nmental organizations.\n\nThis bill reduces the level of soil-lead hazard to 200 parts per million\nfor residential zoned areas and 1,000 parts per million for areas zoned\nfor commercial and/or industrial development\n\nThe EPA has not made any changes in the National Ambient Air Quality\nStandards for Lead (NAAQS) which it promulgated on November 12, 2008\n(Federal Register, Vol. 73, No. 219). The EPA revised the acceptable\nlead level of ambient air quality to .15 micrograms per meter squared\n\"to provide requisite protection of public health and welfare.\"\n\nThis bill reduces the acceptable lead level of ambient air quality to\nlevel of lead exposure from 10 micrograms/deciliter to 5\nmicrograms/deciliter, as recommended by the CDC.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nnone\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nnone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nNinety days after passage.\n",
